==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|turbulent}}, from {{der|en|la|turbulentus}}, from {{m|la|turba|t=disorder, tumult, crowd}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtɜːbjələnt/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtɝbjələnt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-turbulent.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{hyph|en|tur|bu|lent}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# violently [[disturbed]] or [[agitated]]; [[tempestuous]], [[tumultuous]]
#: {{ux|en|It is dangerous to sail in '''turbulent''' seas.}}
# being in, or causing, [[disturbance]] or [[unrest]]
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-08-10|volume=408|issue=8848|magazine={{w|The Economist}}|author=Lexington
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/united-states/21583274-new-wave-press-barons-should-not-allow-newspapers-become-niche-products-keeping Keeping the mighty honest]
|passage=The ''[Washington] Post's'' proprietor through those '''turbulent''' [Watergate] days, Katharine Graham, held a double place in Washington’s hierarchy: at once regal Georgetown hostess and scrappy newshound, ready to hold the establishment to account. That is a very American position.}}
#: {{ux|en|The mid-19th century was a '''turbulent''' time in American history.}}

